From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 17:57:39 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: size the geometry heap from measurement

The per-device geometry heap was a fixed 128 MiB placement heap, taken whole
the first time a command buffer draws through it. The size was a placeholder,
not a bound, and a Venus host pays all of it once per guest VkDevice, so the
cost scales with however many devices a guest keeps alive.

It now starts at 16 MiB and grows. The heaviest single command buffer across
the CTS tessellation, geometry, draw and rasterization groups asks the heap
for 1.53 MiB; one mebibyte fails those groups and two passes them, and
sixteen ran a full released mustpass fraction clean. A CTS-only measurement
under-measured real heap demand, though: demand is linear in patches and
primitives, which the API does not bound, and 130 quad patches at level 64
already need 17.2 MB. So the measurement sets where the heap starts, and the
heap grows, at least doubling and in one step to what a draw is known to
need, up to 128 MiB or the starting size, whichever is larger:

- A direct draw whose reservations the host can bound grows it before it is
  recorded and renders on its first submission. The restart unroll reserves
  a known bound. Tessellation is bounded by every patch at the largest level
  the TCS writes when every level the domain reads is a constant, and at the
  level-64 maximum, 132 KB for a quad patch, otherwise; kk_tess_bound.h
  gives each domain's bound at a level. The first heap is created at the
  size the draw that needs it takes, and a multi-draw grows the heap once
  for all its entries. A draw that does not fit beside the earlier draws of
  its render segment moves the segment to the grown heap. A bound past the
  cap grows the heap to the cap and leaves the rest to the GPU.
- A reservation only the GPU can size is refused when it does not fit, and
  its draw loses its output. The heap header counts refusals, and the next
  draw that pins the heap, or the reset of a recording that used it, grows it
  for later command buffers in one step to hold what every refused render
  segment needed: what it reserved plus every size it was refused. The kernel
  that resets the heap for each segment folds the segment before it into the
  header, and the host adds the segment still open. The first loss is logged
  once per device. Vulkan cannot fail a command buffer after it ran short of
  losing the device, so none fails.

A recording pins every heap it reserved from until it is reset, re-recorded
or freed, and a heap that grew is freed with its last pin. A device that
never draws through the heap allocates none.

MESA_KK_HEAP_SIZE_MIB sets the starting size. It only takes 1 to 4095 MiB,
the range the header's 32-bit size can describe, and any other value keeps
sixteen: a count that wrapped the byte size to zero, one Metal refuses and one
past 4 GiB used to crash the first draw that needed the heap or leave the GPU
a bound larger than the heap. A device Metal refuses its first heap keeps
none, and a draw that needs it fails its command buffer's recording and
records nothing instead of dereferencing it. A device Metal refuses a larger
heap keeps the one it has.

kosmickrisp_heap_size_override draws a triangle fan, which is unrolled through
the heap, under each kind of bad value and under a valid one.
kosmickrisp_heap_growth runs each case on a fresh device and reports the heap
it leaves and the most heap memory it held at once. A 24 MB fan and 130 and
256 quad patches at level 64, whether the TCS writes the level as a constant
or not, render whole on the first submission when drawn directly and hold
only the 32 or 64 MiB heap they leave. Drawn indirectly, the first
submission loses them and reports it, and the second renders them whole. 100
and then 120 patches, which each fit 16 MiB but not together, both render on
the first submission, as do the same patches in one multi-draw, which holds
only 32 MiB. At a constant level 1, 256 patches and 500 draws of 64 stay at
16 MiB; at a level the compiler cannot see, the 500 draws grow the heap in
steps to 128 MiB and hold 240 MiB at most. A device that draws a triangle
list has no heap, and one whose fan fits keeps 16 MiB. Six indirect draws of
39 patches at level 64 and then one of 64 lose their last four on the
first submission, and the second renders them whole: the render pass needs
what the first three reserved plus every refused size, more than either
the largest refused end or the refused sizes alone.

kosmickrisp_heap_overflow_gpu checks that the heap's reset folds a segment's
reserved and refused units into the need, keeps a larger need, and
saturates a sum past 2^32.

kosmickrisp_tess_bound_gpu tessellates patches at levels up to each of 19
maxima from 0.5 to 64, for every domain, partitioning and point mode, and
checks that none exceeds the bound kk_tess_patch_bound() gives.

